# Tor Controller

**A terminal-based Tor connection manager for Windows**

Start, stop, and monitor Tor from a single interactive menu.

[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg)](#)
[![Node](https://img.shields.io/badge/node-v14+-green.svg)](#)
[![Platform](https://img.shields.io/badge/platform-Windows-lightgrey.svg)](#)

---

</div>

## Features

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Start / Stop / Restart** | Full lifecycle control of the Tor process |
| **New Identity** | Switch to a fresh circuit with a new exit IP |
| **Exit IP Lookup** | Query your public exit IP through the SOCKS5 proxy |
| **Circuit Path** | Visualize active circuits: Entry -> Hops -> Exit |
| **Network Stats** | Uptime, bandwidth, traffic, and network status |
| **Live Dashboard** | Auto-refreshing real-time stats (every 3s) |
| **Graceful Shutdown** | Clean Tor termination on Ctrl+C or Q |

## Quick Start

### 1. Clone the repo

```bash
git clone git@github.com:Lexcoded3/Tor.git
cd Tor
```

### 2. Ensure vendor files exist

The `vendor/` directory should contain:

```
vendor/
  nodejs/         # Node.js v14.16.1 runtime
    node.exe
    node_modules/
  tor-bundle/     # Tor 0.4.5.7 binary
    tor.exe
    *.dll
```

If missing, download from:

| Component | Download |
|-----------|----------|
| Node.js | https://nodejs.org/dist/v14.16.1/node-v14.16.1-win-x86.zip |
| Tor | https://www.torproject.org/dist/torbrowser/10.0.15/tor-win32-0.4.5.7.zip |

### 3. Run

```bash
# Double-click run.bat, or:
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js
```

## Menu

```
  ╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  │                       M A I N   M E N U                         │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  [1]  Start TOR connection                                      │
  │  [2]  Log connection info                                       │
  │  [3]  Stop TOR connection                                       │
  │  [4]  Debug TOR connection                                      │
  │  [5]  New TOR identity                                          │
  │  [6]  Restart TOR connection                                    │
  │  [7]  Show exit IP                                              │
  │  [8]  Show circuit path                                         │
  │  [9]  Network stats                                             │
  │  [0]  Live dashboard                                            │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  Press a key (1-9, 0) or Q to quit                              │
  ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
```

## CLI Mode

Run commands directly without the interactive menu:

```bash
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 1    # Start Tor
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 5    # New identity
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 7    # Show exit IP
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 9    # Network stats
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 0    # Live dashboard
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js q    # Quit
```

## How It Works

```
┌─────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐
│   run.js    │────>│  tor.exe     │────>│  Tor Network│
│  (Node.js)  │     │  (SOCKS:9050)│     │             │
│             │<────│  (Ctrl:9051) │<────│             │
└─────────────┘     └──────────────┘     └─────────────┘
```

- **Process Management** -- Tor is spawned as a child process and tracked in memory
- **Control Protocol** -- Port 9051 via `tor-control` library for signals and queries
- **SOCKS5 Proxy** -- Port 9050 for HTTP requests through Tor (exit IP detection)
- **Zero Dependencies** -- Uses only Node.js built-in modules + vendored packages

## Project Structure

```
Tor/
  run.js           # Main application
  run.bat          # Windows launcher
  torrc            # Tor configuration
  README.md        # This file
  .gitignore       # Excludes vendor binaries
  vendor/
    nodejs/        # Bundled Node.js runtime
    tor-bundle/    # Bundled Tor binary + DLLs
```

## Configuration

Edit `torrc` to customize Tor behavior:

```
SocksPort 9050        # SOCKS proxy port
ControlPort 9051      # Control port (for this tool)
Log notice stdout     # Log level
```

## Troubleshooting

| Issue | Solution |
|-------|----------|
| **Ports already in use** | Stop other Tor instances or change ports in `torrc` |
| **GeoIP warnings** | Non-fatal, can be ignored |
| **Batch file closes immediately** | Run from terminal to see error output |
| **Connection timeout** | Check firewall, try restarting Tor |

